21:["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-the-eviction-question",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-the-eviction-question","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"The eviction question, and how we measure the answer"}]]}]}],["$","li","02-opt-optimal",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#02-opt-optimal","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"02"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"OPT, the optimal policy you can never build"}]]}]}],["$","li","03-fifo",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#03-fifo","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"03"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"FIFO, evict the oldest, and pay for it"}]]}]}],["$","li","04-beladys-anomaly",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#04-beladys-anomaly","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"04"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Belady’s anomaly, more memory, more faults"}]]}]}],["$","li","05-lru",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#05-lru","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"05"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"LRU, bet that the recent past predicts the near future"}]]}]}],["$","li","06-lru-approximations",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#06-lru-approximations","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"06"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Approximating LRU with a single reference bit"}]]}]}],["$","li","07-clock-algorithm",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#07-clock-algorithm","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"07"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"The Clock algorithm, second-chance as a spinning hand"}]]}]}],["$","li","08-frame-allocation",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#08-frame-allocation","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"08"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"The knobs around eviction: allocation, dirty pages, prefetch"}]]}]}],["$","li","09-reality-engineers",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#09-reality-engineers","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"09"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Reality: what real kernels do, and why you already know this"}]]}]}]]}]
22:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-the-eviction-question",{"id":"01-the-eviction-question","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The eviction question, and how we measure the answer"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Here is the exact situation. Demand paging means a page is only brought into RAM when it is actually touched. A process runs, touches a page that is not resident, and faults. The kernel needs a free physical frame to load that page into. If the free list is empty, every frame is occupied, the kernel cannot proceed until it frees one. It runs the page replacement algorithm: pick a resident page (the victim), write it out to disk if it has been modified, mark its page-table entry as not-present, and hand the now-free frame to the faulting page. Only then does the faulting instruction get to retry."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Notice the stakes hidden in that paragraph. Every eviction is a bet about the future: you are betting the victim page will not be needed again soon. If you lose that bet, the very next reference to the victim faults, and you have paid two disk trips (writing it out, reading it back) to accomplish nothing. The goal of a replacement algorithm is therefore blunt: minimise the number of page faults over the life of the program. Fewer faults means fewer disk trips means the process spends its time computing instead of waiting."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"To reason about this precisely we need a way to talk about a program’s memory behaviour without the noise of a whole address space. We use a reference string: the sequence of page numbers a process touches, in order. Real programs touch millions of pages, but the interesting behaviour of an algorithm shows up on a short string, so that is what we trace by hand. We fix the number of physical frames available, feed the reference string in one page at a time, and at each step ask: is this page already resident (a hit) or not (a fault)? On a fault with no free frame, the algorithm chooses a victim."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Reference string"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The ordered sequence of page numbers a process accesses; the standard way to describe and compare replacement policies."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Frame"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A fixed-size slot of physical RAM that holds exactly one page. The number of frames is what limits how much of the process fits in memory."}]]}],"$L24","$L25","$L26"]}],"$L27","$L28"]}],"$L29"]}]]}],"$L2a","$L2b","$L2c","$L2d","$L2e","$L2f","$L30","$L31"]}]
23:["$","$L32",null,{"prev":{"slug":"virtual-memory-demand-paging","title":"Virtual Memory & Demand Paging","kind":"$undefined","number":23},"next":{"slug":"paging-design-thrashing","title":"Paging Design & Thrashing","kind":"$undefined","number":25}}]
